= 1
{parent=2008/A}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2008/A/1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/A/1/a}

Hall's theorem states that a finite <bipartite graph> with classes $L,R$ has a <matching> covering $L$ if and only if $|N(U)|\ge|U|$ for every $U\subseteq L$. Here $N(U)$ is the set of vertices of $R$ adjacent to at least one member of $U$. Necessity follows because matched partners of $U$ are distinct members of $N(U)$.

For sufficiency induct on $|L|$; zero or one vertex is immediate. If a nonempty proper $U\subset L$ has $|N(U)|=|U|$, Hall's condition holds within the <graph> on $U,N(U)$. It also holds on the remaining classes: for $T\subseteq L\setminus U$,
$$
|N(T)\setminus N(U)|=|N(T\cup U)|-|N(U)|\ge|T|.
$$
Induction gives matchings in these two disjoint subgraphs, and their <union> covers $L$. Otherwise every nonempty proper $U$ has $|N(U)|\ge|U|+1$. Choose any edge $lr$ and delete its two endpoints. Every nonempty subset of the remaining left class was a proper subset of $L$, so deleting $r$ leaves at least as many neighbours as vertices. Induction gives a <matching> there, and adjoining $lr$ completes the <matching>. This proves sufficiency in all cases.

= b
{parent=2008/A/1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/A/1/b}

Make a <bipartite graph> whose two classes index the two partitions, joining $i$ on the left to $j$ on the right exactly when $A_i\cap B_j\ne\varnothing$. For a subset $U$ of left indices, its <union> of blocks contains $2008|U|$ elements. They all lie in the blocks indexed by $N(U)$, whose <union> has $2008|N(U)|$ elements. Thus $|N(U)|\ge|U|$. Hall's theorem gives a <matching> covering all $n$ left vertices, hence also all $n$ right vertices. Its partner map is the required permutation $\pi$.

= c
{parent=2008/A/1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/A/1/c}

In the vertex form of <Menger's theorem>, for distinct nonadjacent vertices $s,t$ of a finite <graph>, the maximum number of pairwise internally vertex-disjoint $s$–$t$ paths equals the minimum size of an $s$–$t$ <vertex separator>. A separator is a subset of vertices other than $s,t$ whose deletion leaves no $s$–$t$ path; internally disjoint paths may share their endpoints but no other vertices. Equivalently, between disjoint vertex sets the maximum number of mutually vertex-disjoint connecting paths equals the minimum size of a separating vertex set, where endpoints may be deleted in that set version.

= d
{parent=2008/A/1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/A/1/d}

Add $s$ adjacent to every vertex of $V_1$ and $t$ adjacent to every vertex of $V_2$. Suppose an $s$–$t$ separator $C$ has fewer than $n$ vertices. It cannot contain all of either class. Choose $x\in V_1\setminus C$ and $y\in V_2\setminus C$. No edge can join the remaining two classes, since it would give a three-edge $s$–$t$ path. Hence every neighbour of $x$ lies in $C\cap V_2$, and every neighbour of $y$ lies in $C\cap V_1$. The minimum-degree bound implies
$$
|C|\ge|C\cap V_1|+|C\cap V_2|\ge n/2+n/2=n,
$$
a contradiction. <Menger's theorem> therefore gives $n$ internally vertex-disjoint $s$–$t$ paths. Each uses at least one vertex of each original class, and only $n$ vertices are available in each class. Every path must consequently use exactly one of each. Their middle edges form **a <perfect matching>**.

= 2
{parent=2008/A}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2008/A/2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/A/2/a}

The <Turán graph> $T_r(n)$ is the complete $r$-partite <graph> whose nonempty part sizes differ by at most one. Write $n=qr+s$, $0\le s<r$: it has $s$ parts of size $q+1$ and $r-s$ parts of size $q$.

Turán's theorem states that every simple $n$-vertex <graph> with no $K_{r+1}$ has at most $e(T_r(n))$ edges, with equality precisely for a <graph> isomorphic to $T_r(n)$. Its edge number is half of $n^2$ minus the sum of the squared part sizes.

= b
{parent=2008/A/2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/A/2/b}

For a positive <integer> $x$, $p_G(x)$ counts proper vertex colorings using a labelled palette of $x$ colors. Choose an edge $e=uv$. Proper colorings of $G-e$ divide into those in which $u,v$ have different colors, counted by $p_G(x)$, and those in which they have the same color, counted by $p_{G/e}(x)$. Thus
$$
p_G(x)=p_{G-e}(x)-p_{G/e}(x),
$$
where parallel edges after contraction are suppressed. Induction, using $p_G(x)=x^{|V(G)|}$ for an edgeless <graph>, produces a <polynomial> agreeing with every <integer> coloring count. The <polynomial> is unique because two polynomials agreeing at infinitely many integers are identical.

= c
{parent=2008/A/2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/A/2/c}

Deletion-contraction also proves the alternating-sign form with nonnegative coefficients. If $G$ has $n$ vertices, $G/e$ has $n-1$, so subtracting its alternating expansion adds its coefficient at each succeeding index:
$$
a_i(G)=a_i(G-e)+a_{i-1}(G/e).
$$
Starting from an edgeless <graph> gives $a_0=1$ and no constant term when $n>0$.

For the first coefficients, inclusion-exclusion over the forbidden monochromatic edges gives the precise expression
$$
p_G(x)=\sum_{F\subseteq E(G)}(-1)^{|F|}x^{c(F)},
$$
where $c(F)$ counts components of the spanning <graph> $(V(G),F)$, including isolated vertices. Only $F=\varnothing$ gives $x^n$. A rank-one edge set is a single edge, giving coefficient $-m$ at $x^{n-1}$. Rank two comes from every two-edge subset, each with positive sign, and every triangle, each with negative sign. No other simple edge set has rank two: its nontrivial components must be a three-vertex component or two separate edges. Thus
$$
\boxed{a_0=1,\qquad a_1=m,\qquad a_2=\binom m2-t}.
$$
The displayed $a_2$ assertion concerns $n\ge3$; below this degree the corresponding coefficient is absent and its expression is zero.

= d
{parent=2008/A/2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/A/2/d}

The degree of a <chromatic polynomial> is the number of vertices and the negative of its next coefficient is the number of edges. Equality with $p_{K_n}$ therefore forces $n$ vertices and $\binom n2$ edges. A simple <graph> on those vertices with that many edges contains every possible edge, so $\boxed{G\cong K_n}$.

= e
{parent=2008/A/2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/A/2/e}

The <chromatic number> is the smallest positive <integer> $q$ for which $p_G(q)>0$. Thus equality of the polynomials implies $\chi(G)=r$, as well as $|V(G)|=n$ and $e(G)=e(T_r(n))$. In particular $G$ contains no $K_{r+1}$, since such a <clique> requires $r+1$ colors. The equality case in Turán's theorem now gives $\boxed{G\cong T_r(n)}$. This uses more than equality of triangle counts: the <polynomial> also determines colorability.

= 3
{parent=2008/A}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2008/A/3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/A/3/a}

The <Ramsey number> $R(s,t)$ is the least $N$ such that every $N$-vertex <graph> contains a <clique> of size $s$ or an <independent set> of size $t$. Take $N=R(s-1,t)+R(s,t-1)$ and choose a vertex $v$. Among its $N-1$ other vertices, either it has at least $R(s-1,t)$ neighbours or it has at least $R(s,t-1)$ nonneighbours.

In the first case those neighbours contain an independent $t$-set or an $(s-1)$-<clique>, which joins $v$ to form an $s$-<clique>. In the second case the nonneighbours contain an $s$-<clique> or an independent $(t-1)$-set, which joins $v$ to form an independent $t$-set. This proves
$$
\boxed{R(s,t)\le R(s-1,t)+R(s,t-1)}.
$$

= b
{parent=2008/A/3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/A/3/b}

The five-cycle and its complement both have no triangle, so $R(3,3)>5$. In a six-vertex <graph>, any vertex has at least three neighbours or three nonneighbours. In the first triple an edge completes a triangle with the chosen vertex, while no edges give an independent triple. The complementary argument handles three nonneighbours. Hence **$R(3,3)=6$**.

Now suppose a nine-vertex <graph> contains no triangle and no independent four-set. Each vertex has at most three neighbours, since its neighbourhood is independent. It cannot have at most two neighbours: its at least six nonneighbours contain, by the result just proved, either a triangle or an independent triple; the former is forbidden and the latter joins the vertex to give an independent four-set. Every degree must therefore equal three. But nine vertices of degree three have odd total degree, contradicting the handshake lemma. Thus **$R(3,4)\le9$**.

= c
{parent=2008/A/3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/A/3/c}

The <graph Ramsey number> $R(G,H)$ is the least $N$ such that every red-blue coloring of the edges of $K_N$ contains a red copy of $G$ or a blue copy of $H$. Copies are ordinary subgraphs, not necessarily induced. Equivalently every <graph> on $N$ vertices contains $G$ or its complement contains $H$.

= d
{parent=2008/A/3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/A/3/d}

Assume the usual nontrivial case $\chi(G)\ge2$ and $|H|\ge2$. On $(\chi(G)-1)(|H|-1)$ vertices partition into $\chi(G)-1$ equal classes of size $|H|-1$. Color all edges between distinct classes red and all edges within a class blue. The red <graph> is $(\chi(G)-1)$-colorable, so it contains no copy of $G$. Every blue <connected> component has only $|H|-1$ vertices, so <connectedness> prevents a blue copy of $H$. Therefore one more vertex is necessary:
$$
\boxed{R(G,H)\ge(\chi(G)-1)(|H|-1)+1}.
$$
If $G$ is edgeless or $H$ has one vertex, the displayed lower bound is just one and holds directly.

= 4
{parent=2008/A}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2008/A/4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/A/4/a}

For nonempty disjoint vertex sets, the edge density is $d(X,Y)=e(X,Y)/(|X||Y|)$. A pair is $\epsilon$-uniform, also called $\epsilon$-regular, if every $X'\subseteq X$ and $Y'\subseteq Y$ with $|X'|\ge\epsilon|X|$ and $|Y'|\ge\epsilon|Y|$ satisfies
$$
|d(X',Y')-d(X,Y)|<\epsilon.
$$
It says that restricting to reasonably large subsets does not noticeably change the edge density.

= b
{parent=2008/A/4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/A/4/b}

In the standard regularity-partition convention, the vertex sets form a disjoint partition, the nonexceptional sets $V_1,\ldots,V_k$ are nonempty and have a common size, $|V_0|<\epsilon|V(G)|$, and at most $\epsilon k^2$ unordered nonexceptional pairs fail to be $\epsilon$-uniform. One can instead use weak inequality for the exceptional-set bound without affecting the argument below once the size bound is strict. The exceptional set absorbs the rounding remainder when the <graph> order is not divisible by $k$.

= c
{parent=2008/A/4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/A/4/c}

Write $N=|G|$ and $D(U)=e(G[U])-p\binom{|U|}{2}$. For disjoint sets $U,W$ of size at least $\epsilon^2N$, the given identity and the corresponding binomial identity give
$$
|e(U,W)-p|U||W||\le|D(U\cup W)|+|D(U)|+|D(W)|<\epsilon^5N^2/3.
$$
In the nonvacuous regularity range $0<\epsilon<1$, the original sets therefore satisfy $|d(X,Y)-p|<\epsilon^3/3$. Their allowed subsets have size at least $\epsilon^2N$, so $|d(X',Y')-p|<\epsilon/3$. The triangle inequality gives
$$
|d(X',Y')-d(X,Y)|<(\epsilon+\epsilon^3)/3<\epsilon.
$$
Thus **the pair is uniform**. For $\epsilon\ge1$, the definition is vacuous or immediate; the subsequent positive-<integer> condition $k<1/\epsilon$ has no solutions.

For the partition deduction, choose $k$ equal classes of size $q=\lfloor N/k\rfloor$, putting the remainder into $V_0$. For fixed $k<1/\epsilon$ and sufficiently large $N$, $q\ge\epsilon N$ and $|V_0|<k<\epsilon N$. Every class pair is uniform by what was just proved, which is stronger than the required condition. For example, it suffices that
$$
N>\max\{k/\epsilon,\ k/(1-\epsilon k)\}.
$$
**A <graph>-size qualification is necessary if the printed deduction is read literally for every <graph> order.** Under the standard nonempty-class definition, a one-vertex <graph> satisfies the discrepancy hypothesis for every $p$ and positive $\epsilon$, but cannot be partitioned into two nonempty classes even when $2<1/\epsilon$. The source omits this small-order qualification. If $k$ divides $N$, no rounding is needed and $N\ge k$ already suffices, since $N/k>\epsilon N$.

= B
{parent=2008}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Probabilistic Combinatorics}

= 5
{parent=2008/B}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2008/B/5}
{scope}

= i
{parent=2008/B/5/a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/B/5/a/i}

For $k\ge1$, monotonicity of the logarithm gives
$$
\log(k!)=\sum_{j=1}^k\log j\ge\int_1^k\log t\,dt=k\log k-k+1\ge k\log k-k.
$$
Thus $k!\ge(k/e)^k$. Bounding the numerator of the <binomial coefficient> by $n^k$ yields
$$
\boxed{\binom nk\le\frac{n^k}{k!}\le(en/k)^k}.
$$

= ii
{parent=2008/B/5/a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/B/5/a/ii}

The <function> $x-\log(1+x)$ has derivative $x/(1+x)$, is zero at zero, and is nonnegative for every $x>-1$. Therefore $\log(1+x)\le x$. Multiplying by $m>0$ and exponentiating gives
$$
\boxed{(1+x)^m\le e^{xm}}.
$$
Equality occurs at $x=0$.

= b
{parent=2008/B/5}
{scope}

= i
{parent=2008/B/5/b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/B/5/b/i}

Each of the $\binom n2$ unordered vertex pairs has two independent choices of orientation. Hence the number of tournaments on the specified labelled vertex set is
$$
\boxed{2^{\binom n2}}.
$$

= ii
{parent=2008/B/5/b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/B/5/b/ii}

Orient each edge independently and uniformly. Fix a $k$-set $A$. An outside vertex beats every member of $A$ with probability $2^{-k}$. For different outside vertices these events depend on disjoint sets of edges, so they are independent. Thus
$$
\mathbb P(A\text{ is undominated})=(1-2^{-k})^{n-k}.
$$
A <union bound> over all $\binom nk$ possible sets gives
$$
\mathbb P(\text{some }k\text{-set is undominated})\le\binom nk(1-2^{-k})^{n-k}<1.
$$
The complementary event has positive probability, proving **a <tournament> with the required domination property exists**. This applies in the intended nonvacuous range $n\ge k$; the asymptotic construction below has $n>k$.

= iii
{parent=2008/B/5/b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/B/5/b/iii}

For large $k$, choose
$$
n=\left\lceil 2^k\{k^2\log_e2+2k\log_e k\}\right\rceil.
$$
Using the two inequalities proved in part (a), the logarithm of the <union bound> is at most
$$
k\log_e(en/k)-(n-k)2^{-k}.
$$
Now $\log_e(en/k)=k\log_e2+\log_e k+O(1)$, whereas $(n-k)2^{-k}=k^2\log_e2+2k\log_e k+o(1)$. Their difference is
$$
-k\log_e k+O(k)<0
$$
for all sufficiently large $k$. The existence criterion therefore applies. Since the chosen $n$ satisfies
$$
n=k^22^k(\log_e2)\left(1+O\!\left(\frac{\log k}{k}\right)\right),
$$
we obtain $\boxed{f(k)\le k^22^k(\log_e2)(1+o(1))}$, with the usual convention that the <tournament> has at least $k$ vertices. If orders below $k$ were permitted, the property would be vacuous there; the same upper bound remains valid but the extremal definition would be uninteresting.

= 6
{parent=2008/B}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2008/B/6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/B/6/a}

Explore the individuals one at a time, maintaining the number of discovered but unprocessed individuals. It starts at $S_0=1$. Processing one individual removes it and adds its offspring, so
$$
S_j=1+\sum_{i=1}^j\xi_i,\qquad
\mathbb P(\xi_i=1)=p,\quad\mathbb P(\xi_i=-1)=1-p.
$$
The offspring choices make these increments independent. Exploration stops when the queue first becomes empty; every processed individual has then been counted exactly once. Hence
$$
\boxed{K=\inf\{j\ge1:S_j=0\}},
$$
with $K=\infty$ if the walk never hits zero. The increment sequence can be extended independently beyond the stopping time for probability estimates.

= b
{parent=2008/B/6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/B/6/b}

The event $K=k$ implies $S_k=0$. If $B_k$ counts the positive increments, then $B_k\sim\operatorname{Bin}(k,p)$ and $S_k=1+2B_k-k$. Let $\delta=|p-1/2|>0$.

For $p>1/2$, $S_k=0$ forces $B_k=(k-1)/2\le k/2$, so the lower-tail Hoeffding form of the <Chernoff bound> gives
$$
\mathbb P(K=k)\le\mathbb P(B_k-kp\le-\delta k)\le e^{-2\delta^2k}.
$$
For $p<1/2$ and $k\ge1/\delta$, the required value is at least $kp+\delta k/2$. The upper-tail bound gives $\mathbb P(K=k)\le e^{-\delta^2k/2}$.

To include all smaller positive integers, note that each finite-time atom has probability strictly less than one: at $k=1$ its probability is $1-p<1$, while for $k>1$ immediate extinction already has positive probability and excludes $K=k$. Over the finitely many small $k$ with nonzero atom, take the minimum of $-k^{-1}\log\mathbb P(K=k)$ and the applicable tail constant. This minimum is positive, and zero atoms impose no restriction. Thus for both cases there is $c>0$ such that
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(K=k)\le e^{-ck}\quad\text{for every positive integer }k}.
$$
In the supercritical case this bounds the finite atoms, not the positive probability of $K=\infty$.

= c
{parent=2008/B/6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/B/6/c}

Let $Z_r$ be the size of generation $r$, with $Z_0=1$. Its offspring mean is $2p$, so conditioning on the previous generation gives $\mathbb EZ_r=(2p)^r$. At $p=1/2$,
$$
\boxed{\mathbb EZ_r=1\text{ for every }r,\qquad\mathbb EK=\sum_{r\ge0}\mathbb EZ_r=\infty}.
$$
The interchange uses <Tonelli's theorem> for nonnegative variables. Critical extinction is nevertheless almost sure: its probability is the smallest root in $[0,1]$ of $s=(1+s^2)/2$, whose only root is one. Hence $K$ is finite almost surely. If all its finite atoms had the exponential bound, then $\mathbb EK\le\sum_{k\ge1}ke^{-ck}<\infty$, a contradiction.

For $p>1/2$ the extinction probability is $(1-p)/p<1$. There is positive mass at $K=\infty$, so the mean is infinite even if every finite atom decays exponentially. This is precisely why the critical contradiction does not extend to the supercritical case.

= 7
{parent=2008/B}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2008/B/7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/B/7/a}

The general <Lovász local lemma> concerns events $A_1,\ldots,A_m$ with a dependency <graph>: each $A_i$ is independent of the <sigma-algebra> generated by all nonneighbouring events other than itself. If numbers $x_i\in[0,1)$ satisfy
$$
\mathbb P(A_i)\le x_i\prod_{j\in\Gamma(i)}(1-x_j),
$$
then $\mathbb P(\bigcap_i A_i^c)\ge\prod_i(1-x_i)>0$.

Its symmetric form states that if $\mathbb P(A_i)\le q$, every dependency degree is at most $d$, and $eq(d+1)\le1$, then there is positive probability that no event occurs. For $d\ge1$, set all $x_i=1/(d+1)$. Since
$$
\left(1-\frac1{d+1}\right)^d\ge e^{-1},
$$
we have $x_i(1-x_i)^d\ge1/[e(d+1)]\ge q$, so the general form applies. The displayed inequality follows from $d\log(1+1/d)\le1$. When $d=0$, the events are mutually independent and each has probability below one, so their complements have positive joint probability directly.

= b
{parent=2008/B/7}
{scope}

= i
{parent=2008/B/7/b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/B/7/b/i}

Each row sum is a sum of $k$ independent random signs. For $s>0$, its moment generating <function> is
$$
\mathbb Ee^{sY_j}=(\cosh s)^k\le e^{ks^2/2}.
$$
For example, $\log\cosh s\le s^2/2$ follows by integrating $\tanh s\le s$ for $s\ge0$. <Markov's inequality> yields $\mathbb P(Y_j\ge t)\le\exp(-st+ks^2/2)$. Choosing $s=t/k$ gives $e^{-t^2/(2k)}$ for $t>0$. The distribution is symmetric, so the same bound applies to its negative tail. A <union bound> proves
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(|Y_j|\ge t)\le2e^{-t^2/(2k)}}
$$
for $t\ge0$, including the trivial endpoint $t=0$.

= ii
{parent=2008/B/7/b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/B/7/b/ii}

Let $t=\sqrt{2k\log_e(6kr)}$ and define $A_j=\{|Y_j|\ge t\}$. The preceding estimate gives $\mathbb P(A_j)\le1/(3kr)$. An event depends only on the $k$ signs in that row. Each such column occurs in at most $r-1$ other rows, so its dependency degree is at most $d=k(r-1)$. Events on disjoint sets of signs are independent even jointly, giving the required dependency <graph>.

For positive $k,r$,
$$
e\frac{d+1}{3kr}=\frac{e\{k(r-1)+1\}}{3kr}\le\frac e3<1.
$$
The symmetric <Lovász local lemma> therefore gives positive probability that no $A_j$ occurs. Such a sign vector satisfies
$$
\boxed{\max_j|(Mx)_j|<\sqrt{2k\log_e(6kr)}}.
$$
The positive-parameter assumption is implicit in the printed square-root expression. If all rows are zero, all row sums vanish and no probabilistic construction is needed.

= 8
{parent=2008/B}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2008/B/8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/B/8/a}

<Chebyshev's inequality> says that for a random variable $Z$ with finite mean and <variance>, and every $t>0$,
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(|Z-\mathbb EZ|\ge t)\le\frac{\operatorname{Var}Z}{t^2}}.
$$

= b
{parent=2008/B/8}
{scope}

= i
{parent=2008/B/8/b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/B/8/b/i}

For each cyclic starting position $i$, let $I_i$ indicate that the next $k$ entries are all one. Then $X=\sum_iI_i$ and $\mathbb EI_i=p^k$, hence $\mathbb EX=np^k$.

For two starts separated by $j\in\{1,\ldots,k-1\}$ along either direction, their runs overlap in $k-j$ positions, so $\mathbb E(I_iI_{i+j})=p^{k+j}$. All other distinct starts use disjoint entries and are independent. Since $k\le n/2$, the two directions' overlap ranges do not intersect. Summing the variances and covariances gives
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Var}X=np^k(1-p^k)+2n\sum_{j=1}^{k-1}(p^{k+j}-p^{2k})}.
$$
In particular, dropping negative terms and summing a <geometric series> yields
$$
\operatorname{Var}X\le np^k\left(1+2\sum_{j\ge1}p^j\right)=\mathbb EX\left(1+\frac{2p}{1-p}\right)\le3\mathbb EX
$$
when $p\le1/2$. The formula also covers $k=1$ with an empty <covariance> sum.

\Image[2008-overlapping-runs.png]
{title=Overlap controls the joint success probability}
{description=An original illustrative pair of length-five windows shifted by two on a sixteen-position circle. Their union has seven positions, so both succeed with probability p to the seventh power.}

= ii
{parent=2008/B/8/b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/B/8/b/ii}

For $p=1/2$ and $k=\log_2n+\omega(n)$,
$$
\mathbb EX=n2^{-k}=2^{-\omega(n)}.
$$
If $\omega(n)\to\infty$, the mean tends to zero. <Markov's inequality> gives $\mathbb P(X\ge1)\le\mathbb EX\to0$, so $\boxed{\mathbb P(X=0)\to1}$.

= iii
{parent=2008/B/8/b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/B/8/b/iii}

Now $\omega(n)\to-\infty$ makes $\mathbb EX=2^{-\omega(n)}\to\infty$. <Chebyshev's inequality> and the <variance> bound give
$$
\mathbb P(X=0)\le\frac{\operatorname{Var}X}{(\mathbb EX)^2}\le\frac3{\mathbb EX}\longrightarrow0.
$$
For every fixed $\epsilon>0$, they likewise give
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P\left(\left|\frac X{\mathbb EX}-1\right|>\epsilon\right)\le\frac3{\epsilon^2\mathbb EX}\longrightarrow0}.
$$
Thus the count is concentrated about its growing mean, despite the overlapping runs.

= iv
{parent=2008/B/8/b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/B/8/b/iv}

For $p=1/4$, use $k=\log_4n+\omega(n)$. Then $\mathbb EX=4^{-\omega(n)}$, so its limits are exactly those needed for the same two arguments.

For $p=1/\log_e n$, a convenient definition that also accounts for the varying probability is
$$
\boxed{k=\frac{\log_e n+\omega(n)}{\log_e\log_e n}}.
$$
It makes $\mathbb EX=e^{-\omega(n)}$. Thus $\omega\to+\infty$ gives no runs with high probability, and $\omega\to-\infty$ gives runs and relative concentration. For sufficiently large $n$, $p\le1/2$, so the same uniform <variance> bound applies. Definitions are made from the actual <integer> $k$; no <continuous> rounding assumption is required. These conclusions retain the stipulated range $1\le k\le n/2$.
